  "year": "1983",
  "subtitle": "A pocket translator you operate like a scroll-wheeled library card catalog — lock the first two letters, then scroll the list, then press 'reveal'.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The alpha 8's query model is unlike the QWERTY-translators (Craig M100) or phonetics-based reference devices (Franklin Spelling Ace) already in the museum. There is no full keyboard. You enter a word by pressing two alphabet keys — ABC1 then ABC2 — to lock the first two letters, then scroll an alphabetized word list with up/down search arrows until the target appears, then press 'Übers.' to reveal the translation. It adds a Memo word list and an 'Üben' spaced-practice quiz mode. Sharp built it for the Langenscheidt publishing house in 1983, selling it as a combined calculator + bilingual dictionary in five language editions (German paired with English, French, Italian, Spanish, or Latin). It is a rare pre-Windows handheld in which the 'database query' is performed entirely by mechanical alphabet-stepping and list-scrolling — a physical information-retrieval ritual.",
  "search_terms": ["langenscheidt alpha 8", "langenscheidt alpha8", "langenscheidt electronic dictionary 1983", "sharp langenscheidt translator", "elektronischer vokabel-trainer latein", "langenscheidt vokabel trainer"],
  "research": {
    "title": "Langenscheidt alpha 8",
